  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will maintain influence over the region through\nTuesday, resulting in widespread dry weather over the next\ncouple of days. Patchy to areas of radiation fog are possible \nat inland locations primarily across NE OH/NW PA due to clear \nskies and light winds, but as of now the likelihood of \nsignificant impacts to visibilities is low. \n\nRain chances will increase as a shortwave/weakening upper low\nlifts north/northeast from the Ohio Valley on Wednesday.\nScattered showers and thunderstorms are possible primarily in\nthe southeastern part of the area Wednesday afternoon/evening,\nbut it's still possible that most locations remain dry until an\nupper trough and frontal boundary approach from the west \nThursday and move across the area on Friday. Will need to keep\nan eye on the potential for heavy rainfall late in the week given\nmean flow parallel to the frontal boundary and forecast dew \npoints in the upper 60s and possibly lower 70s. There's also \nsome potential for organized and perhaps strong thunderstorms at\nsome point late in the week, but confidence in the timing and \nplacement of synoptic features and the resulting thunderstorm \nchances is still low at this point in the forecast cycle. Rain \nchances continue through the upcoming weekend as a series of \ndisturbances potentially cross the area.\n\nTemperatures will be near normal for the next several days with\ntemps possibly increasing to slightly above normal values over\nthe weekend. Highs will be in the 80s each day with overnight\nlows in the 60s, although tonight will be an exception with lows\nin the 50s.",
